<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document">Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tauhid Khan
</span></span></div>

<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Abstract</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Flow Matching (FM) constructs linear conditional probability paths, but the learned <em id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">marginal</em> velocity field inevitably exhibits strong curvature due to trajectory superposition. This curvature severely inflates numerical truncation errors, bottlenecking few-step sampling. To overcome this, we introduce <span id="Sx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Isokinetic Flow Matching (Iso-FM)</span>, a lightweight, <em id="Sx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Jacobian-free</em> dynamical regularizer that directly penalizes pathwise acceleration. By using a self-guided finite-difference approximation of the material derivative <math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Dv/Dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Dv/Dt</annotation></semantics></math>, Iso-FM enforces local velocity consistency without requiring auxiliary encoders or expensive second-order autodifferentiation. Operating as a pure <em id="Sx1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">plug-and-play</em> addition to single-stage FM training, Iso-FM dramatically improves few-step generation. On CIFAR-10 (DiT-S/2), Iso-FM slashes conditional non-OT FID@2 from 78.82 to 27.13—a <span id="Sx1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.9<math id="Sx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> relative efficiency gain</span>—and reaches a best-observed FID@4 of 10.23. These results firmly establish acceleration regularization as a principled, compute-efficient mechanism for fast generative sampling.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="Sx1.F1.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:169.1pt;"><img src="2604.04491v1/assets/trajectories_comparison.png" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/117;" width="234" height="117" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="Sx1.F1.2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="Sx1.F1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a) Baseline FM vs Iso-FM trajectories</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="Sx1.F1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:169.1pt;"><img src="2604.04491v1/assets/curvature_comparison.png" id="Sx1.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/150;" width="234" height="150" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="Sx1.F1.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="Sx1.F1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b) Curvature comparison</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Teaser: Iso-FM suppresses path bending in low-dimensional transport. Left: baseline FM trajectories are more curved than Iso-FM trajectories. Right: Iso-FM yields lower curvature, consistent with reducing the material acceleration <math id="Sx1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Dv/Dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Dv/Dt</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper studies sampling efficiency in flow-based generative modeling through transport dynamics rather than model scaling. Our premise is that few-step quality is governed by the <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamical straightness</em> of the learned velocity field. The key quantity is the material derivative</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}=\partial_{t}v+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}=\partial_{t}v+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which measures acceleration along trajectories. When <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Dv/Dt\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Dv/Dt\|</annotation></semantics></math> is large, coarse ODE integration incurs large truncation error; when it is small, trajectories are closer to constant-velocity transport and are easier to integrate with few NFEs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM targets this mechanism with a Jacobian-free regularizer implemented by one lookahead model evaluation and stop-gradient matching, while preserving the standard FM objective and single-stage training loop. We evaluate Iso-FM as a controlled augmentation to FM (not as an absolute SOTA claim): under matched DiT-S/2 CIFAR-10 settings, conditional non-OT FID@2 improves from 78.82 to 27.13 (2.9<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> relative few-step efficiency gain).
Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Abstract ‣ Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> previews this mechanism: baseline FM trajectories bend more and exhibit higher curvature, while Iso-FM yields straighter, lower-curvature transport.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</span></p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamical Regularization for Flow Matching:</span> We establish a direct theoretical link between the material derivative (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Dv/Dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Dv/Dt</annotation></semantics></math>) of the learned Eulerian velocity field and the truncation error experienced during few-step ODE integration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jacobian-Free Optimization:</span> We introduce Isokinetic Flow Matching (Iso-FM), a lightweight, plug-and-play regularizer that penalizes pathwise acceleration. We propose a self-guided finite-difference approximation that requires only standard forward evaluations and stop-gradient targets, entirely avoiding expensive Jacobian-vector products.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improved Few-Step Efficiency:</span> We empirically demonstrate that Iso-FM shifts the Pareto front for single-stage Flow Matching. On CIFAR-10 with a DiT-S/2 backbone, Iso-FM achieves a 2.9<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> relative efficiency gain at NFE=2 (FID 78.82 <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 27.13), proving that acceleration regularization effectively straightens generative flows without requiring multi-stage distillation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Flow Matching and Eulerian Generative Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative flow models aim to construct a time-dependent vector field <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> that pushes a simple prior distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a standard Gaussian) to a complex data distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The evolution of the probability density <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> along this vector field is governed by the continuity equation:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}p_{t}+\nabla\cdot(p_{t}v_{t})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}p_{t}+\nabla\cdot(p_{t}v_{t})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The ideal training objective would directly match the neural network <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> to the true marginal vector field <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> that generates the target probability path <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{marginal}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1),x\sim p_{t}(x)}[\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{marginal}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1),x\sim p_{t}(x)}[\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">However, computing the marginal vector field <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and sampling directly from the marginal path <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are computationally intractable for complex datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> bypasses this intractability by conditioning the vector field on individual source-target pairs. Given a source sample <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a target data sample <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\sim p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\sim p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can construct a simple conditional probability path <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x|x_{0},x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x|x_{0},x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the Optimal Transport (OT) formulation, this path is defined as a linear interpolation:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By differentiating Equation <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Flow Matching and Eulerian Generative Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with respect to time, the corresponding conditional vector field is simply the constant velocity between the two endpoints:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x_{t}|x_{0},x_{1})=x_{1}-x_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x_{t}|x_{0},x_{1})=x_{1}-x_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The fundamental theorem of Flow Matching demonstrates that regressing against this easily computable conditional vector field provides identical gradients to the intractable marginal objective. This yields the standard FM loss:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\mathbb{E}_{t,x_{0},x_{1},x_{t}}[\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x_{0})\|^{2}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\mathbb{E}_{t,x_{0},x_{1},x_{t}}[\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x_{0})\|^{2}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">While the individual conditional trajectories (Equation <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Flow Matching and Eulerian Generative Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) are perfectly straight, the neural network <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> does not have access to the endpoints during inference. Instead, it learns to approximate the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">marginal</em> velocity field, which is the expectation over all possible conditional fields that pass through a given point <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x,t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1}\sim p(x_{0},x_{1}|x_{t})}[x_{1}-x_{0}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1}\sim p(x_{0},x_{1}|x_{t})}[x_{1}-x_{0}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Because independent couplings of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> cause many distinct conditional trajectories to intersect in the state space, this expectation averages out conflicting velocity directions. As a direct consequence, the resulting marginal vector field <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits strong geometric curvature, necessitating multi-step numerical integration during sampling. This is a fundamental bottleneck for few-step generation, and motivates the need for dynamical regularization to suppress pathwise acceleration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Flow Straightening and Acceleration Control</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several works address trajectory curvature to enable faster sampling. <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Rectified Flow</em> iteratively refines the coupling <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> by using samples from a pre-trained model as new data pairs, effectively ”straightening” the flow via recursive training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
Similarly, Optimal Transport Conditional Flow Matching (OT-CFM) minimizes trajectory curvature by matching source and target samples within a minibatch using optimal transport, creating simpler and straighter ground-truth paths for the model to learn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
In <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Minimizing Trajectory Curvature of ODE-based Generative Models</em>, Lee et al. explicitly connect solver error to path curvature and propose minimizing trajectory curvature by untangling path intersections through improved data-noise pairing with a <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE-style encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
More recently, <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">OAT-FM</em> applies optimal transport constraints to minimize acceleration <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\ddot{x}\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\ddot{x}\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> as a post-training refinement step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
<em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Shortcut Models</em> generalize this by conditioning the network on a step-size parameter <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, learning a ”shortcut” <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(x_{t},t,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(x_{t},t,d)</annotation></semantics></math> that predicts the state <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+d}</annotation></semantics></math> directly. They train via a self-consistency recurrence where a step of size <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math> must match two steps of size <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x_{t},t,2d)\approx\frac{1}{2}(s(x_{t},t,d)+s(x_{t+d},t+d,d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x_{t},t,2d)\approx\frac{1}{2}(s(x_{t},t,d)+s(x_{t+d},t+d,d))</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Unlike Iso-FM, Shortcut Models require variable-step conditioning and do not strictly enforce local stationarity of the velocity field itself.
While these methods successfully straighten paths by modifying source-target coupling (a Lagrangian approach), Iso-FM achieves straightness in the Eulerian regime by regularizing the learned velocity field directly, without auxiliary encoders or requiring near-optimal couplings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Flow Map and Consistency Methods</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consistency Models (CM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and Flow Map Matching (FMM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> learn the <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lagrangian solution operator</em> <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{s\to t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{s\to t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> directly. The core objective enforces that points along a trajectory map to the same origin or endpoint:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{CM}=\mathbb{E}[\|f_{\theta}(x_{t+\Delta t},t+\Delta t)-f_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\|^{2}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{CM}=\mathbb{E}[\|f_{\theta}(x_{t+\Delta t},t+\Delta t)-f_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\|^{2}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Recent extensions build upon these concepts to straighten flows.
<em id="S2.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Consistency Flow Matching</em> (CFM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>
enforces self-consistency in the velocity field using a hybrid objective:
a velocity-consistency penalty plus a terminal-consistency loss that encourages
shared endpoint prediction (e.g., <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) along trajectories.
It also evaluates consistency between <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> with an EMA target
network, inheriting the optimization dynamics of consistency training.
Similarly, <em id="S2.SS3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MeanFlow</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>
modifies the regression target by introducing an average velocity</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s\to t}=\frac{1}{t-s}\int_{s}^{t}v_{\tau}d\tau," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s\to t}=\frac{1}{t-s}\int_{s}^{t}v_{\tau}d\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and enforcing an identity that relates <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Position of Iso-FM.</span>
Iso-FM distinguishes itself by operating purely in the <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Eulerian</em> regime without learning an explicit integral map <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> or conditioning on step size <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead, it regularizes the <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">material derivative</em> <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Dv/Dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Dv/Dt</annotation></semantics></math> of the velocity field directly using a finite-difference approximation.
Compared with Consistency Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which emphasizes cross-time velocity consistency as a primary training paradigm, Iso-FM is used as a <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">plug-and-play, Jacobian-free regularizer</em> on top of standard FM regression. In this sense, Iso-FM is orthogonal rather than competitive in setup: it preserves single-stage FM training and can be composed with broader flow-straightening pipelines.
Iso-FM occupies a distinct position: it neither replaces the FM objective nor learns solution maps. Instead, it modifies the <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">geometry of the learned velocity field</em> by suppressing pathwise acceleration, thereby making the induced ODE integrator-friendly.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background: Eulerian and Lagrangian Perspectives</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Continuous-time generative models can be analyzed through two complementary mathematical lenses, borrowed from fluid dynamics: the Eulerian and the Lagrangian perspectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Understanding this duality is crucial for contextualizing the computational bottlenecks of current models and justifying the design of Isokinetic Flow Matching.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Eulerian Perspective (Instantaneous Velocity).</span>
The Eulerian viewpoint observes the flow at fixed spatial coordinates over time. In generative modeling, this corresponds to learning a time-dependent vector field <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> that dictates the instantaneous rate of change of a sample. Data generation is performed by solving the corresponding Initial Value Problem (IVP) through numerical integration:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{dx(t)}{dt}=v(x(t),t),\quad x(0)=x_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{dx(t)}{dt}=v(x(t),t),\quad x(0)=x_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> is fundamentally Eulerian. It trains a neural network <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> to regress the instantaneous target velocity at specific coordinates. While Eulerian objectives are stable and simple to optimize in a single stage, they suffer during inference: if the learned vector field contains complex geometry or crossing paths, the ODE solver requires many small steps (high NFE) to bound the discretization error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Lagrangian Perspective (Particle Trajectory).</span>
Conversely, the Lagrangian viewpoint tracks the continuous trajectory of individual particles (samples) as they move through the state space. Mathematically, this is formalized by the solution operator or flow map <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, which maps an initial state directly to a future state:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{s\to t}(x_{s})=x_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{s\to t}(x_{s})=x_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Recent acceleration techniques, such as Consistency Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and Flow Map Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, adopt a Lagrangian approach. By training a network to explicitly approximate <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{s\to t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{s\to t}</annotation></semantics></math>, they allow for one-step or few-step sampling by directly jumping across time. However, learning global Lagrangian flow maps is notoriously unstable and difficult, often requiring complex multi-stage distillation pipelines, exponential moving average (EMA) teacher networks, and carefully tuned discretization schedules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bridging the Gap: The Role of Pathwise Acceleration.</span>
We introduce this dichotomy to highlight the core theoretical motivation of Iso-FM. The transition between the Eulerian vector field and the Lagrangian trajectory is governed by <em id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">acceleration</em>. If an Eulerian velocity field has zero acceleration along its own flow lines, a particle’s velocity remains constant, and the corresponding Lagrangian flow map becomes trivially linear:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{0\to t}(x_{0})=x_{0}+t\cdot v(x_{0},0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{0\to t}(x_{0})=x_{0}+t\cdot v(x_{0},0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This insight dictates our methodology. Rather than abandoning the stable Eulerian training paradigm to learn explicit Lagrangian flow maps, Iso-FM operates purely in the Eulerian regime. By directly suppressing the pathwise acceleration of the vector field, Iso-FM implicitly induces near-linear Lagrangian transport maps. This approach allows us to approach the few-step sampling efficiency characteristic of Lagrangian methods while retaining the lightweight, single-stage training dynamics of Eulerian Flow Matching.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key observation:</span>
If the Eulerian velocity field has <em id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">zero (or small) acceleration along trajectories</em>, then the flow map becomes approximately linear:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}(x_{0})\approx x_{0}+t\,v(x_{0},0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}(x_{0})\approx x_{0}+t\,v(x_{0},0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM exploits this fact: by straightening the Eulerian field, it implicitly induces simple Lagrangian transport maps without learning them explicitly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Isokinetic Flow Matching (Iso-FM)</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Dynamical Motivation: Acceleration and Sampling Error</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> follow the learned ODE:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}(t)=v(x(t),t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}(t)=v(x(t),t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The acceleration of a particle moving through the field is given by the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">material derivative</span>:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}=\frac{\partial v}{\partial t}+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}=\frac{\partial v}{\partial t}+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A Taylor expansion of the trajectory yields:</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(1)=x(0)+v(x_{0},0)+\frac{1}{2}\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x_{0},0)+\mathcal{O}(\cdot)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(1)=x(0)+v(x_{0},0)+\frac{1}{2}\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x_{0},0)+\mathcal{O}(\cdot).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, one-step Euler error is dominated by the acceleration term. High curvature and acceleration force numerical solvers to take many small steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion:</span>
Few-step generation requires suppressing <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}</annotation></semantics></math> along trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>The MeanFlow Identity (Optional Connection)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MeanFlow-style formulations introduce interval-averaged velocity <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s\to t}=\frac{1}{t-s}\int_{s}^{t}v_{\tau}d\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s\to t}=\frac{1}{t-s}\int_{s}^{t}v_{\tau}d\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and relate it to instantaneous velocity through</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}=U_{s\to t}+(t-s)\frac{d}{dt}U_{s\to t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}=U_{s\to t}+(t-s)\frac{d}{dt}U_{s\to t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This identity motivates temporal consistency objectives. In contrast, Iso-FM directly regularizes the local material acceleration of the Eulerian field via a finite-difference surrogate, while preserving the original FM regression target.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Practical Self-Guided Iso-FM</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM replaces explicit derivatives with a <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">self-guided finite-difference approximation</span>. For a small <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define a forward Euler step using the model’s own velocity:</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+\varepsilon}=x_{t}+\varepsilon\,v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+\varepsilon}=x_{t}+\varepsilon\,v_{\theta}(x_{t},t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Acceleration is approximated by enforcing temporal consistency of velocity predictions:</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Iso}}=\left\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\text{sg}\!\left[v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)\right]\right\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Iso</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.812em;" width="0.812em"><mtext>sg</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Iso}}=\left\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\text{sg}\!\left[v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)\right]\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This loss penalizes changes in velocity <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">along the model’s own trajectory</em>, directly suppressing convective acceleration.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Algorithmic Training Step.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Algorithmic Training Step. ‣ 4.3 Practical Self-Guided Iso-FM ‣ 4 Isokinetic Flow Matching (Iso-FM) ‣ Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the training step and is placed here
to directly accompany the practical self-guided objective.
The implementation follows <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">isofm/core/flow.py</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Isokinetic Flow Matching (Iso-FM) Training Step</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Data <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, noise <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, model <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, weights <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{FM},\lambda_{Iso}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{FM},\lambda_{Iso}</annotation></semantics></math>, exponent <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\text{LogitNormal}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>LogitNormal</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\text{LogitNormal}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sample time step</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Conditional path</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}=x_{1}-x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}=x_{1}-x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Target velocity</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Standard Flow Matching Branch </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{curr}=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{curr}=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\|v_{curr}-u_{t}\|^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\|v_{curr}-u_{t}\|^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Isokinetic Regularization (Jacobian-Free) </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\sim p(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\sim p(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sample small lookahead step</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+\varepsilon}=x_{t}+\varepsilon\cdot\text{stop\_gradient}(v_{curr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>stop_gradient</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+\varepsilon}=x_{t}+\varepsilon\cdot\text{stop\_gradient}(v_{curr})</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{next}=v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{next}=v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=(1-t)^{\alpha}/\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=(1-t)^{\alpha}/\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Temporal weighting</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\|\text{stop\_gradient}(v_{curr})\|_{2}+\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mtext>stop_gradient</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\|\text{stop\_gradient}(v_{curr})\|_{2}+\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Stability constant <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{Iso}=w\cdot\left\|\frac{v_{curr}-\text{stop\_gradient}(v_{next})}{s}\right\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>stop_gradient</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></mfrac><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{Iso}=w\cdot\left\|\frac{v_{curr}-\text{stop\_gradient}(v_{next})}{s}\right\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.15" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{FM}\mathcal{L}_{FM}+\lambda_{Iso}\mathcal{L}_{Iso}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{FM}\mathcal{L}_{FM}+\lambda_{Iso}\mathcal{L}_{Iso}</annotation></semantics></math>


</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Optional OT Path Coupling</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the minibatch Optimal Transport Conditional Flow Matching (OT-CFM) framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, for OT-enabled runs we apply minibatch optimal transport coupling before building the FM interpolation path. Given Gaussian samples <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{0}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{0}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and normalized data <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{1}^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1}^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, we solve a linear assignment problem:</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}=\arg\min_{\pi\in S_{B}}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left\|x_{0}^{(i)}-x_{1}^{(\pi(i))}\right\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}=\arg\min_{\pi\in S_{B}}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left\|x_{0}^{(i)}-x_{1}^{(\pi(i))}\right\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We compute the pairwise squared Euclidean cost matrix and solve the assignment with the Hungarian algorithm, then reorder data and labels using <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Flow Matching and Iso-FM objectives are then applied on</p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+t\,x_{1}^{(\pi^{*})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+t\,x_{1}^{(\pi^{*})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This isolates the effect of improved source-target pairing from the Iso-FM dynamical regularization term.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Interpretation and Path Geometry</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM does not constrain endpoints or enforce map consistency.
Instead, it:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">straightens the Eulerian velocity field,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">reduces kinetic energy variation,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">and implicitly linearizes the induced flow map.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Low-Dimensional Path Geometry Analysis.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide mechanistic evidence for path straightening, we include a controlled low-dimensional comparison between baseline FM and Iso-FM. Let trajectories evolve under the learned velocity field</p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}(t)=v_{\theta}(x(t),t),\qquad\ddot{x}(t)=\frac{Dv_{\theta}}{Dt}=\partial_{t}v_{\theta}+(v_{\theta}\cdot\nabla_{x})v_{\theta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}(t)=v_{\theta}(x(t),t),\qquad\ddot{x}(t)=\frac{Dv_{\theta}}{Dt}=\partial_{t}v_{\theta}+(v_{\theta}\cdot\nabla_{x})v_{\theta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM directly penalizes temporal velocity variation along model trajectories, which reduces <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Dv_{\theta}/Dt\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Dv_{\theta}/Dt\|</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore suppresses path bending.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For geometric quantification, we report a curvature proxy</p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(t)=\frac{\|\ddot{x}(t)\|}{\|\dot{x}(t)\|^{2}+\varepsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(t)=\frac{\|\ddot{x}(t)\|}{\|\dot{x}(t)\|^{2}+\varepsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> ensures numerical stability at low speed. Since <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{x}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{x}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the material acceleration, lower acceleration under Iso-FM implies lower curvature and a straighter transport map.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:169.1pt;"><img src="2604.04491v1/assets/trajectories_comparison.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/117;" width="234" height="117" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:169.1pt;"><img src="2604.04491v1/assets/curvature_comparison.png" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/150;" width="234" height="150" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Low-dimensional geometric diagnostics of learned flows. Left (trajectory comparison): baseline FM paths exhibit stronger bending and non-uniform directional changes, while Iso-FM trajectories are more linear between source and target regions. Right (curvature comparison): the Iso-FM model maintains consistently lower curvature magnitude across integration time, empirically supporting the theoretical objective of acceleration suppression.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, Iso-FM incurs minimal memory overhead and integrates seamlessly into standard FM training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Terminology</h3>

<div id="S4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The term <span id="S4.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isokinetic</span> denotes approximately preserved speed along trajectories. Directional changes are allowed; rapid acceleration and curvature are discouraged.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments use a DiT-S/2 backbone on CIFAR-10 across conditional/unconditional and OT/non-OT settings. In OT runs, source-target pairing is computed with minibatch Hungarian matching on squared Euclidean cost before FM interpolation. Metrics are recorded every 250 epochs. We report only the best score per metric, together with its corresponding epoch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation objective and claim scope.</span>
Our experimental objective is not to establish a new absolute state-of-the-art on CIFAR-10; rather, it is to isolate and quantify the effect of Eulerian acceleration regularization on sampling speed under controlled single-stage training. Accordingly, all comparisons are made between FM baselines and Iso-FM-augmented models with matched architecture, training horizon, and evaluation protocol.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Training Configuration</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Training Configuration ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the exact optimization and model settings used for the CIFAR-10 runs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Training configuration for the main CIFAR-10 experiment.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:103.5pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Item</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:217.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Value</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:103.5pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.4.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Backbone</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:217.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">DiT-S/2 Transformer (depth </span><math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, hidden dim </span><math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=384</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, heads </span><math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, patch size </span><math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, MLP ratio </span><math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=4.0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">).</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:103.5pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.4.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Trainable parameters</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
