<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix A: Theory of Isokinetic Flow Matching</h2>

<section id="Ax1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">A.1 Eulerian Dynamics and Material Acceleration</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Ax1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x,t):\mathbb{R}^{d}\times[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t):\mathbb{R}^{d}\times[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth time-dependent vector field.
A particle trajectory <math id="Ax1.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> follows the ODE</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{dx(t)}{dt}=v(x(t),t),\quad x(0)=x_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{dx(t)}{dt}=v(x(t),t),\quad x(0)=x_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The acceleration of the particle is the total derivative of the velocity along the trajectory:</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}v(x(t),t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}v(x(t),t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying the chain rule yields the <em id="Ax1.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">material derivative</em>:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x,t)=\frac{\partial v}{\partial t}(x,t)+\left(v(x,t)\cdot\nabla_{x}\right)v(x,t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x,t)=\frac{\partial v}{\partial t}(x,t)+\left(v(x,t)\cdot\nabla_{x}\right)v(x,t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This quantity decomposes acceleration into:</p>
<ul id="Ax1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ax1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local (temporal) acceleration</span> <math id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}v</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convective acceleration</span> <math id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v</annotation></semantics></math> due to spatial variation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A necessary and sufficient condition for straight-line trajectories is</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}\equiv 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}\equiv 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">A.2 Relation to One-Step Integration Error</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a Taylor expansion of the trajectory around <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(1)=x_{0}+v(x_{0},0)+\frac{1}{2}\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x_{0},0)+\mathcal{O}(\|Dv/Dt\|^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(1)=x_{0}+v(x_{0},0)+\frac{1}{2}\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x_{0},0)+\mathcal{O}(\|Dv/Dt\|^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The one-step Euler approximation is</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{1}=x_{0}+v(x_{0},0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{1}=x_{0}+v(x_{0},0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the leading-order integration error satisfies</p>
<table id="Ax1.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x(1)-\hat{x}_{1}\|\;\approx\;\frac{1}{2}\left\|\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x_{0},0)\right\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x(1)-\hat{x}_{1}\|\;\approx\;\frac{1}{2}\left\|\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x_{0},0)\right\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implication.</span>
Accurate few-step or one-step generation requires suppressing the material acceleration of the learned velocity field, particularly at early times.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">—</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">A.3 Self-Guided Finite-Difference Approximation</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Direct evaluation of <math id="Ax1.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}</annotation></semantics></math> requires Jacobian–vector products, which are computationally expensive for large neural networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM replaces this with a <em id="Ax1.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-guided finite-difference estimator</em>.
Given a small <math id="Ax1.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define a forward Euler step using the model’s own velocity:</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+\varepsilon}=x_{t}+\varepsilon\,v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+\varepsilon}=x_{t}+\varepsilon\,v_{\theta}(x_{t},t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A first-order Taylor expansion of the velocity yields</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)+\varepsilon\left(\partial_{t}v_{\theta}+(v_{\theta}\cdot\nabla_{x})v_{\theta}\right)+\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)+\varepsilon\left(\partial_{t}v_{\theta}+(v_{\theta}\cdot\nabla_{x})v_{\theta}\right)+\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Rearranging,</p>
<table id="Ax1.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)-v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)}{\varepsilon}\;\approx\;\frac{Dv_{\theta}}{Dt}(x_{t},t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>ε</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)-v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)}{\varepsilon}\;\approx\;\frac{Dv_{\theta}}{Dt}(x_{t},t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, minimizing</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-v_{\theta}(x_{t+\varepsilon},t+\varepsilon)\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">acts as a stochastic, Jacobian-free surrogate for minimizing the squared material acceleration.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This is the core principle behind <span id="Ax1.SSx3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">self-guided Isokinetic Flow Matching</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">A.4 Effect on Flow Geometry</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If Iso-FM successfully enforces</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv_{\theta}}{Dt}\approx 0\quad\text{along trajectories}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>along trajectories</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv_{\theta}}{Dt}\approx 0\quad\text{along trajectories},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then velocity is approximately constant along each path:</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x(t),t)\approx v_{\theta}(x_{0},0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x(t),t)\approx v_{\theta}(x_{0},0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Integrating the ODE yields</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)\approx x_{0}+t\,v_{\theta}(x_{0},0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)\approx x_{0}+t\,v_{\theta}(x_{0},0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">i.e., the flow map becomes approximately linear in time.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SSx4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key insight.</span>
Iso-FM does not learn flow maps explicitly; instead, it reshapes the Eulerian field so that the induced Lagrangian maps are simple.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">A.5 The Fundamental Limit of Flow Straightening</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Iso-FM successfully minimizes the material acceleration <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}</annotation></semantics></math> to straighten the marginal velocity field, achieving a perfectly linear global Lagrangian flow map (where <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere) is mathematically impossible when matching a simple prior to a complex, multimodal data distribution. We demonstrate this analytically by connecting the marginal acceleration to the conditional velocity variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> be a generalized conditioning variable (e.g., a specific target <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, or a coupled source-target pair <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x_{0},x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x_{0},x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>). Let <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x,t|z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x,t|z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,t|z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,t|z)</annotation></semantics></math> be the conditional probability density and conditional velocity field, respectively. By construction in Optimal Transport Flow Matching and Rectified Flow, the conditional paths are straight, meaning their material acceleration is strictly zero:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Du}{Dt}=\partial_{t}u+(u\cdot\nabla_{x})u=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Du}{Dt}=\partial_{t}u+(u\cdot\nabla_{x})u=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The marginal density <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and marginal velocity <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> are defined via marginalization over the prior distribution <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(z)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Ax1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Ax1.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax1.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax1.E26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int p(x,t|z)q(z)dz," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int p(x,t|z)q(z)dz,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="Ax1.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax1.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax1.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{p(x,t)}\int u(x,t|z)p(x,t|z)q(z)dz=\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[u]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{p(x,t)}\int u(x,t|z)p(x,t|z)q(z)dz=\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[u].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Both the conditional and marginal fields satisfy their respective continuity equations: <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}p(\cdot|z)+\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)u)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}p(\cdot|z)+\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)u)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}p+\nabla_{x}\cdot(pv)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}p+\nabla_{x}\cdot(pv)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We seek to evaluate the material acceleration of the marginal field, <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}=\partial_{t}v+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}=\partial_{t}v+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v</annotation></semantics></math>. We begin by taking the partial time derivative of the marginal momentum <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pv</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}(pv)=\int\partial_{t}\big(p(\cdot|z)u\big)q(z)dz=\int\big(u\partial_{t}p(\cdot|z)+p(\cdot|z)\partial_{t}u\big)q(z)dz." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}(pv)=\int\partial_{t}\big(p(\cdot|z)u\big)q(z)dz=\int\big(u\partial_{t}p(\cdot|z)+p(\cdot|z)\partial_{t}u\big)q(z)dz.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Substituting the conditional continuity equation <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}p(\cdot|z)=-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}p(\cdot|z)=-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)u)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}(pv)=\int\Big(-u\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)u)+p(\cdot|z)\partial_{t}u\Big)q(z)dz." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}(pv)=\int\Big(-u\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)u)+p(\cdot|z)\partial_{t}u\Big)q(z)dz.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Using the vector calculus identity <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}\cdot(puu^{T})=u\nabla_{x}\cdot(pu)+p(u\cdot\nabla_{x})u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\cdot(puu^{T})=u\nabla_{x}\cdot(pu)+p(u\cdot\nabla_{x})u</annotation></semantics></math>, we can rewrite the integrand:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}(pv)=\int\Big(-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)uu^{T})+p(\cdot|z)\big[\partial_{t}u+(u\cdot\nabla_{x})u\big]\Big)q(z)dz." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}(pv)=\int\Big(-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p(\cdot|z)uu^{T})+p(\cdot|z)\big[\partial_{t}u+(u\cdot\nabla_{x})u\big]\Big)q(z)dz.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.4" class="ltx_p">By Equation <a href="#Ax1.E25" title="In A.5 The Fundamental Limit of Flow Straightening ‣ Appendix A: Theory of Isokinetic Flow Matching ‣ Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>, the bracketed term is exactly zero. Pushing the integral inside the divergence yields:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}(pv)=-\nabla_{x}\cdot\left(p(x,t)\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[uu^{T}]\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}(pv)=-\nabla_{x}\cdot\left(p(x,t)\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[uu^{T}]\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.5" class="ltx_p">We now apply the Reynolds decomposition from fluid mechanics. The expectation of the uncentered second moment of the conditional velocities can be decomposed into the outer product of the marginal mean velocity plus the conditional covariance matrix <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[uu^{T}]=vv^{T}+\Sigma(x,t),\quad\text{where}\quad\Sigma(x,t)=\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[(u-v)(u-v)^{T}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[uu^{T}]=vv^{T}+\Sigma(x,t),\quad\text{where}\quad\Sigma(x,t)=\mathbb{E}_{z|x_{t}=x}[(u-v)(u-v)^{T}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.6" class="ltx_p">Substituting this decomposition back into the momentum equation yields:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}(pv)=-\nabla_{x}\cdot(pvv^{T})-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p\Sigma)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}(pv)=-\nabla_{x}\cdot(pvv^{T})-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p\Sigma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.7" class="ltx_p">Expanding the left side using the product rule (<math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}(pv)=v\partial_{t}p+p\partial_{t}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}(pv)=v\partial_{t}p+p\partial_{t}v</annotation></semantics></math>) and the first term on the right side (<math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}\cdot(pvv^{T})=v\nabla_{x}\cdot(pv)+p(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\cdot(pvv^{T})=v\nabla_{x}\cdot(pv)+p(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v</annotation></semantics></math>), we obtain:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v\big[\partial_{t}p+\nabla_{x}\cdot(pv)\big]+p\big[\partial_{t}v+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v\big]=-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p\Sigma)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\big[\partial_{t}p+\nabla_{x}\cdot(pv)\big]+p\big[\partial_{t}v+(v\cdot\nabla_{x})v\big]=-\nabla_{x}\cdot(p\Sigma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.8" class="ltx_p">The first bracketed term is identically zero due to the marginal continuity equation. The second bracketed term is exactly the material derivative of the marginal velocity. Dividing by <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we arrive at the fundamental limit equation:</p>
<table id="Ax1.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x,t)=-\frac{1}{p(x,t)}\nabla_{x}\cdot\big(p(x,t)\Sigma(x,t)\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}(x,t)=-\frac{1}{p(x,t)}\nabla_{x}\cdot\big(p(x,t)\Sigma(x,t)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Physical Implication.</span>
Equation <a href="#Ax1.E35" title="In A.5 The Fundamental Limit of Flow Straightening ‣ Appendix A: Theory of Isokinetic Flow Matching ‣ Isokinetic Flow Matching for Pathwise Straightening of Generative Flows" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a> proves that the marginal acceleration <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly governed by the spatial divergence of the conditional velocity variance <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> (the “kinetic stress tensor”). At early times (<math id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>), the prior <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is highly uninformative, meaning a single spatial coordinate <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has a non-zero probability of routing to widely separated data modes. Thus, the posterior variance <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(x,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(x,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Because the probability density <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> must decay to zero at spatial infinity (<math id="Ax1.SSx5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>), the non-negative tensor field <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x,t)\Sigma(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x,t)\Sigma(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> must also vanish at infinity. A smooth field that decays to zero at boundaries cannot have a globally zero divergence unless the field itself is identically zero. Therefore, <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}\cdot(p\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\cdot(p\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be globally zero, meaning the material acceleration <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Dv}{Dt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Dv}{Dt}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="Ax1.SSx5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">must</em> be non-zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Physically, a particle drawn from the prior must accelerate to “commit” to a specific data mode as <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> and the posterior collapses. Therefore, while Iso-FM successfully suppresses arbitrary convective acceleration, a perfectly linear global transport map is theoretically unachievable unless the data-to-noise coupling is entirely deterministic and one-to-one (<math id="Ax1.SSx5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>), which violates the independent or minibatch-OT coupling formulations of standard generative flows.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix B: Relation to Flow Maps and Solution Operators</h2>

<section id="Ax2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.1 Eulerian vs. Lagrangian Objectives</h3>

<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Ax2.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the solution operator (flow map) induced by <math id="Ax2.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Ax2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}(x_{0})=x(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}(x_{0})=x(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="Ax2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ax2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Eulerian learning</span> (Flow Matching): learn <math id="Ax2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> locally.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lagrangian learning</span> (Flow-map / consistency methods): learn <math id="Ax2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> directly.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Learning <math id="Ax2.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> collapses the ODE into a direct mapping but removes intermediate-time semantics and requires additional supervision or distillation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM remains Eulerian but modifies the learned field so that <math id="Ax2.SSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes trivial to approximate numerically.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.SSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">—</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.2 Learning Velocity and Flow Maps Jointly (Preliminary)</h3>

<div id="Ax2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the equivalence</p>
<table id="Ax2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\Phi_{t}(x)=v(\Phi_{t}(x),t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\Phi_{t}(x)=v(\Phi_{t}(x),t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">one may consider learning both:</p>
<ul id="Ax2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ax2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a velocity field <math id="Ax2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a flow map <math id="Ax2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\psi}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\psi}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with a consistency constraint</p>
<table id="Ax2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\psi}(x_{t},t)\;\approx\;\Phi_{\psi}(x_{t}+\varepsilon v_{\theta}(x_{t},t),t+\varepsilon)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\psi}(x_{t},t)\;\approx\;\Phi_{\psi}(x_{t}+\varepsilon v_{\theta}(x_{t},t),t+\varepsilon).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this view:</p>
<ul id="Ax2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ax2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM regularizes the <em id="Ax2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Eulerian dynamics</em>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the flow-map head learns the corresponding <em id="Ax2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lagrangian operator</em>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Preliminary toy experiments suggest that Iso-FM significantly stabilizes such joint training by reducing stiffness of the velocity field. However, we emphasize that this direction remains exploratory and is not required for few-step gains reported in the main paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix C: Limitations and Open Problems</h2>

<div id="Ax3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Isokinetic Flow Matching (Iso-FM) provides a principled and lightweight mechanism for reducing trajectory curvature and enabling efficient few-step sampling, several limitations and open questions remain.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">C.1 Limits of One-Step Generation</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM substantially reduces material acceleration of the learned velocity field, which in turn lowers discretization error for coarse numerical solvers. However, this alone does not guarantee high-fidelity <em id="Ax3.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">one-step</em> generation in complex, multimodal data distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, when the conditional posterior <math id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{1}\mid x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{1}\mid x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly multimodal at early times, the optimal marginal velocity remains an average over competing directions. While Iso-FM suppresses rapid changes in this average along trajectories, it does not resolve the fundamental ambiguity at <math id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Empirically, this manifests as a performance gap between <math id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-step and <math id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-step sampling that persists even under strong regularization.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This limitation is intrinsic to instantaneous velocity regression and suggests that true one-step generation may require either:
(i) explicit flow-map learning,
(ii) additional symmetry-breaking mechanisms,
or (iii) auxiliary conditioning information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">C.2 No Guarantee of Global Non-Intersection</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM enforces low acceleration <em id="Ax3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally along trajectories</em>, but does not impose a global constraint preventing different trajectories from intersecting in state space.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although intersections become exponentially unlikely in high-dimensional spaces, particularly for image data, a formal guarantee of global non-intersection remains open. Understanding whether additional weak regularization terms could promote global topological consistency without sacrificing efficiency is an important direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">C.3 Hyperparameter Sensitivity</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The effectiveness of Iso-FM depends on several hyperparameters, including the isokinetic weight <math id="Ax3.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, the time-weighting exponent <math id="Ax3.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and the distribution used to sample the lookahead step <math id="Ax3.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While we find that log-normal or Beta-distributed <math id="Ax3.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> schedules are robust across tasks, a principled method for selecting these parameters remains an open problem. Adaptive schemes based on local curvature, posterior entropy, or velocity variance may offer a more automatic alternative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">C.4 Relation to Flow-Map Learning</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM modifies the Eulerian dynamics so that the induced Lagrangian flow maps become approximately linear and easy to integrate numerically. However, Iso-FM does not explicitly learn these maps.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work on flow-map learning and consistency models demonstrates that directly learning solution operators can achieve true one-step generation. A precise theoretical characterization of when Iso-FM suffices to recover these operators implicitly—and when explicit flow-map learning is necessary—remains unresolved.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our preliminary toy experiments suggest that combining Iso-FM with lightweight flow-map heads may be promising, but we leave a systematic investigation of this hybrid regime to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">C.5 Theoretical Generalization Bounds</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While we provide local Taylor-based arguments linking Iso-FM to reduced Euler integration error, global generalization guarantees for long-time transport remain open.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, bounding the accumulated error of few-step solvers as a function of the expected material acceleration along learned trajectories is an important theoretical question that may connect Iso-FM to recent advances in neural ODE stability analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">C.6 Scalability to Very Large Models</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM introduces minimal memory overhead and avoids second-order derivatives, making it compatible with large architectures such as DiT. However, the interaction between isokinetic regularization and optimization dynamics at scale—especially under mixed-precision training and aggressive optimizer settings—requires further study.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Understanding how Iso-FM interacts with architectural inductive biases, attention mechanisms, and normalization layers is another promising direction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">C.8 Broader Perspective</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM represents a step toward <em id="Ax3.SSx7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamically informed generative modeling</em>, where local physical constraints on motion shape global generative behavior. We believe that future work at the intersection of generative modeling, numerical analysis, and dynamical systems will continue to yield both theoretical insights and practical speedups.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Takeaway</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Iso-FM provides a principled mechanism for straightening generative flows at the dynamical level.
Once acceleration is suppressed, learning an explicit solution operator becomes optional rather than necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax3.SSx8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this sense, Iso-FM bridges Eulerian and Lagrangian generative modeling by enforcing conditions under which they coincide.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax4" class="ltx_appendix">
